def build_memory_weights_graph():
    """
    Teaches the weights.txt localized token importance ledger:
    format, per-file sections, update mechanics, distinction from neural weights.
    Also reads live weights.txt to include actual top words if available.
    """
    lines = [
        "<|user|> What is weights.txt and how is it formatted? <|endoftext|>",
        (
            "<|assistant|> weights.txt is the localized semantic token-importance ledger. "
            "It is not a neural network parameter file. "
            "Format: a section header in square brackets for each named memory filename "
            "such as [dictionary.txt] followed by tab-separated word and value pairs "
            "one per line sorted descending by value. "
            "Example: [facts.txt] then verified tab 12 then confirmed tab 8. "
            "The same word can have different weights in different sections "
            "because importance is local to each semantic store. <|endoftext|>"
        ),
        "<|user|> How does weights.txt get updated? <|endoftext|>",
        (
            "<|assistant|> weights.txt is updated by update_memory_weights on every read and write "
            "of a named memory file. "
            "Writes increment word frequencies by 1.0 per token. "
            "Reads increment by 0.1 so reading a file has a smaller weight impact than writing. "
            "Reclassification events increment by 1.5 for the destination file "
            "to emphasise the newly promoted content. "
            "The ledger is rewritten atomically via a temporary file to prevent corruption. "
            "weights rebuild recalculates from scratch from current file contents. <|endoftext|>"
        ),
        "<|user|> How does the training pipeline use weights.txt? <|endoftext|>",
        (
            "<|assistant|> train_base_multimodal_decayv2.py loads weights.txt from dgapi/system/ "
            "and generates a training record from its content with a MEMORY_WEIGHTS_LEDGER tag. "
            "train_m_K_e.py similarly reads the ledger and includes it in the knowledge engine corpus "
            "so the multi-bank model understands that word importance differs by semantic store. "
            "The live ledger is used by the orchestrator's semantic_memory_context_for_prompt "
            "to score which memory files are most relevant to the current prompt. <|endoftext|>"
        ),
    ]

    # Include actual top weights if available
    weights_path = "session.noq/data/memory/weights.txt"
    if os.path.exists(weights_path):
        try:
            weights_text = open(weights_path, encoding="utf-8").read().strip()
            if weights_text:
                lines.append("<|user|> What are the current top weights in weights.txt? <|endoftext|>")
                lines.append(
                    f"<|assistant|> [MEMORY_WEIGHTS_SAMPLE] {weights_text[:1000]} <|endoftext|>"
                )
        except Exception:
            pass

    return "\n".join(lines)

def build_computation_age_graph():
    """Teach age measured in computations rather than dates."""
    lines = [
        "<|system|> AItech computation-age map. Age is measured in computations, not in wall-clock time. One computation is one user prompt processed through the terminal front end. <|endoftext|>",
        _qa("What is a computation?",
            "One computation is one user prompt processed through the terminal front end. It is the unit in which AItech measures age. The count lives in session.noq/dgapi/system/computations.txt and advances once per submitted prompt, whether that prompt was conversation or a command."),
        _qa("Why measure age in computations instead of dates?",
            "Because what matters is how much thinking has happened since something was written, not how much the clock moved. A machine switched off for a month has aged nothing: it processed nothing. Computation age is also stable where dates are not, surviving timezone changes, daylight saving, clock skew, and long idle periods."),
        _qa("Is a creation date kept at all?",
            "Yes, as a starting reference. Each tracked memory file records the epoch second at which it was first stamped, in the [@borndate] section, so absolute time stays recoverable when a person needs it. Tokens and entries do not carry their own dates; they are aged in computations against the file's reference."),
        _qa("Where is age recorded?",
            "In weights.txt, the same ledger that already holds per-file token importance. Five @-prefixed sections were added: [@computations] for the current tick and the genesis timestamp, [@born] for the tick at first sighting, [@borndate] for the creation reference, [@seen] for the tick of last access, and [@uses] for how many times something was touched."),
        _qa("How are the aging keys shaped?",
            "A memory filename alone for the file itself, filename::token for a token inside that file, and filename::#hash for one entry inside that file. One flat number per key, which is what the existing ledger format expects, so nothing about how weights.txt is read had to change."),
        _qa("Why does the aging data live in the ledger rather than in the memory files?",
            "Because the memory stores carry their own block structures: triage blocks, reclassification records, provenance headers. Writing age tags into them would collide with those and would mean rewriting a semantic store on every read. Keeping age in the ledger leaves every store byte-identical and confines the bookkeeping to the file whose job is bookkeeping."),
        _qa("What can be asked of the aging data?",
            "How old a memory file is, how old any entry in it is, and how old the individual tokens making up those entries are, each expressed as the number of computations processed since it was created. The same data answers how many computations have passed since something was last touched, and how many times it has been used."),
        _qa("What does it mean to shear the weights?",
            "Weights grow like a sheep's hair and are trimmed when they stop growing. When an entry becomes computationally complete, its creation date is trimmed away and replaced by the marker <computed>. The marker says the entry is settled: nothing is left to solve for and no date needs carrying."),
        _qa("When is an entry computationally complete?",
            "When everything it refers to has been resolved adequately and no new computation has disturbed it for a quiet stretch of computations. Completeness is about outstanding work, not about correctness: a settled entry is one with nothing left to do, which is not the same as one proven right."),
        _qa("What happens when a settled entry is modified later?",
            "The <computed> marker is replaced by a fresh creation date and the entry's birth tick resets to the current computation. It begins aging again from the moment it became unsettled rather than from when it was first written, because what matters is how long it has been settled, not how long it has existed."),
        _qa("Does reading a settled entry un-shear it?",
            "No. Only a modifying computation regrows a sheared entry. Consulting something is not the same as changing it, so a settled entry that is read stays settled and keeps its marker."),
        _qa("Who does the shearing?",
            "The engine that owns the ledger, during its own atomic save. The disk orchestrator counts the flock and reports how many keys are sheared and how many are still growing, but it does not write the ledger itself: a second writer would race the engines for the same file."),
        _qa("What does the ledger record about computations overall?",
            "The [@computations] section holds now and total, the lifetime count of computations performed, runs, the number of program initializations recorded, session_start, the tick at which the current run began, and genesis, the epoch second when counting began. Computations performed since this initialization are now minus session_start."),
        _qa("Does a high use count make an entry more true?",
            "No. Use count and age describe how a memory has been handled, not whether it is correct. They are organizational signals for deciding what to surface and what has gone quiet. Confidence remains a separate six-state judgement about the content itself."),
        _qa("What does it mean when a file is old but recently touched?",
            "That it was created long ago in the model's own experience and is still in active use: durable material. The opposite pattern, recently created but untouched since, marks something that arrived and was never returned to, which is a candidate for review rather than for reinforcement."),
    ]
