# INTAKE POLICY — how absorbed sources are treated
# =============================================================================
def build_intake_policy_graph():
    """Teach free-use absorption: take meaning, do not retain the work whole."""
    lines = [
        "<|system|> AItech intake policy map. Input files are treated as free use for inspiration. Meaning is absorbed across the knowledge bases; the work itself is not retained whole. <|endoftext|>",

        _qa("How should an input file be treated?",
            "As free use, the way a book author reads a book for inspiration in their own works without duplicating it entirely. Read it, absorb what it means, and let that meaning produce additions across the knowledge bases. Do not retain the work whole."),
        _qa("What should be written when a book is absorbed?",
            "Derived material: a paraphrased digest, vocabulary, entities, relations, observed constructions, uncertainties, and passage references. Derived material carries provenance back to the source so it can be cited without being reproduced."),
        _qa("Where does the derived material live?",
            "In the internal dgapi, in the media folder matching the one the source arrived in, under the source filename plus a (memory) tag. The original moves to the external processed folder. One absorbed file therefore produces one named internal counterpart rather than scattered fragments with no home."),
        _qa("What attribution must the derived file record?",
            "Whatever the document stated: copyright marks, author, title, production or release date, publisher, identifiers, and language, written as a DERIVED FROM QUOTED SOURCE header at the top of the file. Recording attribution is not optional, and inventing it is worse than leaving it UNKNOWN."),
        _qa("What if a source states no author or title?",
            "Record those fields as UNKNOWN. UNKNOWN is distinct from NO: it means the document did not say, not that the document has no author. A later edition, a user statement, or a related source may supply the field, at which point the header can be completed."),
        _qa("What should not be written when a book is absorbed?",
            "A verbatim copy of the source text, and a byte copy of the original file kept alongside the notes. Retaining the work whole is duplication rather than absorption, and it is not what intake is for."),
        _qa("What is the difference between provenance and reproduction?",
            "Provenance records where an understanding came from: source name, timestamp, and passage reference. Reproduction reprints the source itself. Provenance is required. Reproduction is not, and a provenance record is not an excuse for one."),
        _qa("Where do absorbed meanings go?",
            "They are distributed across the knowledge bases according to their semantic roles. A definition may reach definitions, a repeated construction may influence dictionary or gentext stores, a factual claim may reach facts with a confidence state. Absorption is distributed, not filed to one destination."),
        _qa("What happens to the part of a book that was not understood?",
            "It is recreated in the internal dgapi overflow store for interpretation on a later processing loop. Nothing is discarded because a single pass failed to comprehend it. Overflow is how absorption stays lossless without retaining the work whole."),
        _qa("May the librarian quote a source?",
            "A short passage reference for a specific purpose is ordinary scholarly use. Reproducing a work, or reconstructing one in pieces across many replies, is not. The test is whether the output would substitute for the source."),
        _qa("Does free use mean the source has no claim?",
            "No. Free use here describes how AItech absorbs material for its own learning. It does not license redistribution of the source, and it does not remove the obligation to record where an understanding came from."),
    ]
    return "\n".join(lines)


